"""sen0401_ch03_deck_notes v1_0_0: the reader-facing notes of the chapter 3 deck (one per slide, 60 to 140 words, full sentences, no presenter directions, no everyday analogy)
and the literal replacements for the text blocks of deck v3.2.0 that carried an analogy (owner ruling of 2026-11-24). Used by sen0401_ch03_deck_fix_v1_0_0.py and sen0401_ch03_page_fix_v1_0_0.py.
Slide numbers are those of deck v3.2.0 (the slide count does not change)."""
__version__ = "1.0.0"

NOTES = {
1: "This chapter opens Bitcoin Core, the program that checks every rule of the network. Chapter 2 followed one payment through the network; this chapter reads, builds, runs and questions the program that carries out the checks. The deck has seven parts, three true stories and nineteen code examples, and each example has a twin on the course page. Every number was computed by the chapter library from saved data, and no Bitcoin Core node was run for the examples. The picture shows a coin turning into code, taken from the course's 2025 deck.",
2: "The route lists nine numbered items: the questions and the chapter map, seven parts that lead from the source code to the toolkits around a running node, and the summary with the resources and the closing. Each item names the slides it covers, so any range can be opened directly. The three counters give the size of the deck: sixty-one slides, three true stories and nineteen code examples. Every code example prints its result under the listing and shows it as a picture. A reader may keep one question in mind for each part and look for its answer on the slides.",
3: "Six questions frame the chapter, and the summary returns to each of them with a one-line answer. The first asks what makes Bitcoin Core the reference implementation; it is a question about authority and not about history, because the program defines the rules by running them. The second asks how to choose a release, check it and build it. The third covers the settings that decide what a node stores, remembers and trusts. The fourth covers asking a running node questions and who may ask. The fifth covers decoding a transaction, and the sixth checking a block by hand and the programs that can talk to a node.",
4: "The map shows the chapter as six steps along one program: read the source code, build it, run it, ask it questions through its interface, read transactions and blocks, and reach it from libraries and other clients. The parts of the deck follow these steps in order. The nineteen code examples run along this path, and each prints its result under the listing and shows it as a picture beside it. A reader who is lost can return to this slide to see which step a part belongs to and which step comes next.",
5: "Part 2 introduces three terms. A reference implementation is the program against which other programs are measured: other software is correct when it agrees with it. A full verification node downloads every block and checks every rule itself, and it trusts no summary from other nodes. A Bitcoin Improvement Proposal is a numbered public document that proposes a change to Bitcoin; BIP 9, met later in this chapter, describes version bits. A common mistake is to call Bitcoin Core the only possible node. It is the reference, and other clients exist, which part 8 returns to.",
6: "On 8 January 2009 Satoshi Nakamoto announced Bitcoin 0.1 on the metzdowd.com cryptography mailing list. It was a Windows program that anyone could download from SourceForge and run, and it came five days after the first block had been mined. Only running software could start the network, so the program came before any written rule, and Satoshi later wrote that the core design was set in stone once 0.1 was out. The lesson is that a reference implementation's rules are what that program does. A common mistake is to think the white paper alone defined Bitcoin. The photograph shows a bust of Satoshi in Budapest.",
7: "Bitcoin Core is one program with four parts. The connection manager talks to the peers and passes on blocks and transactions. The validation engine checks every block and every transaction against the rules. The storage engine keeps the headers, the blocks and the set of coins. The RPC part lets other programs ask the node questions, and a wallet uses it. A common mistake is to treat the node as a database only; the validation engine is its core, because a node that stores data without checking it adds nothing to the network. The figure is taken from the course textbook, Mastering Bitcoin.",
8: "A full node follows five steps: it starts, finds peers and keeps a limited number of connections, performs the initial download, checks every block and transaction against every rule, and then stays in sync by accepting and passing on what is valid. The initial download fetches and checks the whole chain before transactions are processed. The chart shows the catch-up cost after being offline: at 400 megabytes a day, a node off for ten days fetches about four gigabytes. A full node's report is authoritative because it verified the data itself. A common mistake is to think a node trusts a peer's summary.",
9: "The version field of a block header is not a counter. Its top three bits mark a version-bits block, as BIP 9 defines, and each lower bit that is set is one proposal's vote. With the value 0x20000002 the top bits read 001, which is the marker, and bit 1 is set, so one proposal signals support. Twenty-nine bits are available, so up to twenty-nine proposals can signal in parallel. The code computes the top bits and the list of set bits, and the table shows all 32 bits with the marker in orange and the vote in teal.",
10: "Open source means the code is published under a licence that lets anyone use, change and distribute it; Bitcoin Core uses the MIT license. A fork is a split of the chain into two branches, by accident or for good when the rules change incompatibly. A soft fork is a backward-compatible tightening of the consensus rules: the blocks that the new rules accept are a smaller set inside the set the old rules accept, so old nodes keep following the chain. BIP 9 lets several soft forks be deployed at once by signalling in the block version. An incompatible change splits the chain in two.",
11: "Part 3 turns source code into programs and introduces three terms. A release tag is a name that fixes one exact version of the source, such as v31.1, so the same tag always gives the same source. A release candidate is a tagged version offered for testing before it becomes final, and its tag name carries rc. The program bitcoind is the node itself, which runs in the background. A common mistake is to build a release candidate and call it a release, or to skip the tag and build whatever the main branch holds that day.",
12: "A build has three decisions, which follow the three headings of the book's building section. Selecting a release means checking out the tag of a final release, not of a candidate. Configuring means choosing options, such as whether to build the wallet or the window, and a different choice of options builds a different set of programs. Building runs the compiler, which turns the source into the programs of the bin folder; it can take up to an hour, and the unit tests run after it. A common mistake is to skip the tag and compile the development branch.",
13: "Five steps lead from the source code to bitcoind. A git clone copies the repository with its whole history. The build prerequisites are libraries and tools that must be present before compiling, and a package manager installs them together with the software they depend on. The configure step chooses the options, using the older Autotools build or the current CMake build. Compilation turns the source files into programs. The bitcoin command is a front door to the node and its tools, and its -m option runs the multiprocess programs instead of the monolithic ones.",
14: "These are real tags of the Bitcoin repository, listed on 2026-10-04. Release candidates carry rc in their tag, so the code first removes them and keeps the final releases. Sorted as text, v0.10.0 comes before v0.9.5, because the character 1 sorts before 9. Sorted by number with the library's version key, the oldest final release, v0.9.5, comes first. The table shows both orders side by side. A common mistake is to sort version tags as plain text and conclude that the oldest release is v0.10.0.",
15: "A release archive unpacks into a bin folder of programs and a libexec folder of parts that those programs use. Release 31.1 has seven programs, among them bitcoind, the node, and bitcoin-cli, the client that calls it. Beside bin, libexec holds three parts: bitcoin-gui, bitcoin-node and test_bitcoin. The code counts the entries in each folder and checks that bitcoind is among the programs. A common mistake is to run bitcoin-cli with no node running; the client only forwards calls, so it needs a node that answers them.",
16: "On 13 September 2021 Bitcoin Core released version 22.0, the first release built with Guix, a build system that Carl Dong wrote. Guix lets anyone rebuild the program from the tagged source and get the same file, bit for bit. Each independent builder signs the hashes of what they built, and the signatures are collected in the guix.sigs repository on GitHub. For release 31.1 the SHA256SUMS file carries 11 good signatures, so a download is checked against many builders and not against one person's word. A common mistake is to think a signature proves the code is good; it proves the file matches.",
17: "Each release ships a list of hashes called SHA256SUMS. A user hashes the downloaded archive and compares the result with the listed value; equal texts mean the file is the one that was published. For release 31.1 the listed hash and the computed hash both start with b80d9c3e04da and are equal in all 64 characters. A single changed bit would change the whole hash. A common mistake is to compare only the first characters by eye; the comparison should cover all 64 characters, and the code does exactly that with one equality test.",
18: "A download can be checked in three ways, and each closes one gap. A checksum is a fixed-size value computed from a file by a hash function, and it shows that the file equals the published one. An OpenPGP signature, made with a secret key and checked with the matching public key, shows that the list of checksums comes from the key holder. A reproducible build gives bit-for-bit identical files from the same source, so many independent builders can attest to the same checksums. None of the three proves that the signers deserve trust; release verification means the file matches the published record.",
19: "Part 4 starts the node and sets it up, and introduces three terms. The configuration file is a text file of settings that the node reads when it starts. The data directory is the folder in which the node keeps blocks, state, logs and settings, and a node started with another data directory begins with its own blocks and state. Pruning deletes old block files after the node has checked them, which saves disk space. A common mistake is to prune first and ask for the transaction index later, because the two options cannot be combined.",
20: "A configuration file holds defaults, and an option given on the command line overrides them. The code merges the file's settings first and the command line's last, so where both set the same option the command line wins. Here the file sets dbcache to 450 and turns the transaction index on, while the command line asks for dbcache 2048. The result keeps 2048 for dbcache and txindex 1 from the file. A common mistake is to reverse the order of the merge, which lets the file override the command line.",
21: "One option can come from three places: the command line, the dynamic settings file that the node writes itself, and the configuration file. The command line wins when it sets the option. The start-up log prints the node's version, the settings it read, its connection limits, the interface it opens and the wallet database, so it shows what the node really did. The alertnotify option runs a command of your choice when the node raises an alert, and %s stands for the message. A common mistake is to ignore the log after a change.",
22: "The data directory holds everything the node knows. The table lists the entries of an offline node of release 31.1: banlist.json, bitcoin.conf, bitcoind.pid, the blocks and chainstate folders, debug.log, the demo wallet folder, fee_estimates.dat, mempool.dat, peers.dat and settings.json. The long blocks folder holds the block files and their index, the chainstate folder holds the set of unspent outputs, and debug.log is the node's diary. The .cookie file with the temporary password is written here while the node runs and removed when it stops. A common mistake is to delete the folder to free space.",
23: "The dbcache setting is divided before it is used. With 1,024 MiB the library splits the cache the way Bitcoin Core does: 2 MiB for the block index database, 8 MiB for the chain state database and 1,014 MiB for the coins cache, which sums to 1,024. Nearly all the memory therefore serves the coins cache. The bars show the three parts on one scale, with two very short bars and one very long one. A common mistake is to think that a bigger dbcache grows every database equally.",
24: "Pruning keeps only recent blocks on disk, while the transaction index needs every block. The code checks three option sets: prune alone is accepted, the index alone is accepted, and both together are refused. Release 31.1 recorded this refusal when it was started with both options. The reason is simple: a node cannot index blocks that it has already deleted. Students often expect a quiet downgrade, in which the node drops the index; instead the node stops with an error and the configuration must be changed.",
25: "Two options reduce what a node asks of its machine without giving up verification. The blocksonly option takes blocks from peers but no loose transactions, so the mempool stays almost empty and memory use falls to about five megabytes. The maxmempool option caps, in megabytes, the pool of unconfirmed transactions; the default of 300 MB is 286.1 MiB, about 60 times more than blocks-only mode needs. Transactions above the cap are evicted, lowest fee rate first. A common mistake is to think a smaller memory limit makes a weaker node; the node still checks every block.",
26: "The alertnotify option runs a command of yours when the node raises an alert. A hostile alert text could try to close the quote and run a second command. The node therefore strips every character outside a short safe list before it inserts the message at %s. The code starts from a text with two quote characters, and the cleaned result has them removed: two characters were stripped, so no quote can end the word early. Cleaning is the defence, and it protects the shell command that the node starts.",
27: "Wallets inside the node are described by output descriptors. An output descriptor is a short text that describes a set of output scripts, optionally followed by an 8-character checksum that catches typing and copying errors. A descriptor wallet keeps one descriptor for each output type it supports and is stored in SQLite; it is the current default. A legacy wallet is a Berkeley DB file described by its keys, and since Bitcoin Core 30.0 it can only be migrated. The table lists the address prefixes: P2PKH starts with 1, P2SH-P2WPKH with 3, P2WPKH with bc1q and P2TR with bc1p. An address and a descriptor are not the same thing.",
28: "On 7 April 2014 Gavin Andresen passed the lead-maintainer role of Bitcoin Core to Wladimir van der Laan, who led until 2022. Satoshi Nakamoto had handed the project to Andresen in 2011. Andresen wanted to focus on protocol work, and a reference implementation cannot wait for one person. Every change is reviewed in the open on GitHub, whoever holds the role. The lesson is that the reference implementation is an institution, not a person, and that a team reviewing in public holds it today. A common mistake is to think one person controls Bitcoin Core.",
29: "Part 5 talks to a running node and introduces four terms. The bitcoin-cli tool is a command-line client that calls the node's interface. JSON-RPC is a way to call a program's functions with small JSON messages. Cookie authentication uses a secret that the node writes to a file at every start. The rpcauth option stores a user name with a salted hash of the password. A script on the same machine can read the cookie, while a program that cannot read the data directory needs rpcauth. A common mistake is to open the interface to the whole internet.",
30: "An API is the list of requests a program may make to another program, and the node's list is reached through JSON-RPC over an HTTP request: a POST to 127.0.0.1, port 8332. The stack runs from your program through the API and the JSON-RPC interface to the JSON data format that carries the answer. A JSON call is an object of names and values in curly brackets, and square brackets hold a list, here an empty list of parameters. The bitcoin-cli client sends the same calls the API offers, and its help command lists them. A common mistake is to think the node speaks a special language; it speaks plain HTTP.",
31: "A JSON-RPC call is a small JSON text that names a method and its parameters. The node checks the caller, runs the method and answers with a result, an error and the id of the call. For getblockchaininfo the recorded sample reply of the book reports 799,278 blocks and an empty error, which means the call worked. The id of the call, curltest, comes back unchanged in the reply, so a client can match each answer to its question when several calls are in flight. The code builds the body and reads the method, the block count and the error from the reply.",
32: "At every start bitcoind writes a new random secret into the .cookie file. A client reads the file, joins the user name __cookie__ and the secret with a colon, and sends the result Base64-encoded in a header. The book's example cookie has 64 hexadecimal characters, which stand for 32 bytes, and the encoded header starts with X19jb29raWVf. Because the secret is new each time, nothing has to be invented or stored in advance. A common mistake is to copy the cookie into scripts; the file changes at every start.",
33: "The rpcauth option holds a fixed credential for programs that cannot read the cookie. Its line contains the user name, a random salt and the keyed hash of the password, computed with HMAC-SHA256, and no password. In the example the three parts have 5, 32 and 64 characters. Computing the line again with the same salt and a wrong password gives a different hash, so the comparison is False. A leaked configuration file therefore shows only the hash. A common mistake is to think the salt is a second password; it is a public random value that makes each hash different.",
34: "Two ways let a program prove that it may call the node. The cookie is a random value that the node writes at every start; any program that can read the data directory can call the node. Hashed credentials, set with rpcauth, are fixed: a user name, a random salt of 32 characters and the 64-character HMAC-SHA256 hash of the password, which cannot be reversed. A leaked file shows only the hash. The cookie suits programs that can read the cookie file, and rpcauth suits programs that cannot. A common mistake is to paste the password itself into the configuration.",
35: "This is the answer of a real node of release 31.1 that had downloaded nothing yet. The call getblockchaininfo reports the chain, main, the number of blocks, 0, and the flag initialblockdownload, True. A fresh node knows only the first block, whose hash starts with 000000000019, and it says that it is still downloading. The code reads those three fields from the JSON text. A common mistake is to read difficulty 1 as a malfunction; it is the difficulty of the first block.",
36: "Two status calls report two different things. The call getblockchaininfo describes the chain: its name, the number of blocks and headers, the best block, its difficulty and chain work, and whether the node is still in its initial download. The call getnetworkinfo describes the node's own network side: its version, the protocol version, the services it offers, its connections and reachable networks, and the fee rates it uses to accept transactions. Both are questions, not commands, so asking changes nothing in the node. To learn whether a node is still catching up, use getblockchaininfo.",
37: "Part 6 reads a transaction the way the node reports it, with three terms. The transaction ID, or txid, is the hash that names a transaction and is computed without its witness. Transaction malleability is changing a transaction's id without changing what the transaction does, which segregated witness closed for new transactions by keeping signatures out of the txid. Weight units are the measure by which a block is filled, counting witness bytes once and all other bytes four times. A common mistake is to treat a txid as the transaction itself; it is only its name.",
38: "A function is a small named piece of code that can be used again. The keyword def starts it, then come the name, the inputs in brackets and a colon, and the indented line that follows hands back a value with return. Here sat takes an amount in bitcoin and returns satoshis, the whole number of 10 to the power 8 times the amount. The table shows the two outputs of Alice's transaction passed through it: 0.0002 becomes 20,000 and 0.00075 becomes 75,000. The next slide calls this function. A common mistake is to think the name sat belongs to the amount; it names the function.",
39: "The node's decoder turns Alice's transaction bytes into fields, and decoderawtransaction shows them as JSON in which the amounts are written in bitcoin. The code defines the helper sat, loops over the outputs and returns pairs of output number and satoshis: output 0 is 20,000 and output 1 is 75,000. Output 0 is a witness_v1_taproot output and output 1 a witness_v0_keyhash output, and the size is 143 virtual bytes. The fee of 5,000 satoshis is not a field; it is the difference between inputs and outputs. A common mistake is to read bitcoin amounts as satoshis.",
40: "Serialization writes a transaction as a sequence of bytes, shown in hexadecimal, and decoding reads the same bytes as labelled fields. Alice's transaction has 194 bytes: version 4, marker and flag 2, inputs 42, outputs 75, witness 67 and lock time 4, which add up to 194. The marker and the flag stand before the inputs when a witness follows, and a node reads them to know that a witness is present. The decoded output shows nothing new, only names for the same bytes. A common mistake is to forget the marker and flag when adding the sizes.",
41: "The txid is the hash of the transaction without its witness, and the wtxid is the hash of the transaction with it. For Alice's transaction the txid starts with 466200308696215b and the second hash with f7cdbc7cf8b910d3. They differ because the signature sits in the witness: the txid ignores it, so a changed signature cannot change the txid, and only the second hash changes with it. A common mistake is to think the witness is secret; it is only kept apart from the part the txid covers.",
42: "A block is filled by weight units. The code splits Alice's 194 bytes into 125 base bytes and 69 witness bytes. Base bytes weigh four units each and witness bytes one, so the weight is 4 times 125 plus 69, which is 569, the same number Bitcoin Core reports. A virtual byte is four weight units rounded up, so 569 divided by 4 gives 143 virtual bytes. A common mistake is to add 125 and 69 and call the sum, 194, the weight.",
43: "Segregated witness moves signature data out of the part that the txid covers, into a witness that counts less toward the size limit. The bars show the effect on Alice's transaction: counting every byte four times would give 776 weight units, but the base bytes count 4 times 125, which is 500, and the witness bytes count once, 69, so the weight is 569 and the virtual size 143. A third party can no longer change the txid by re-encoding a signature. A common mistake is to think the witness is thrown away; every node still checks it.",
44: "Part 7 reads blocks, with three terms. The block height is a block's place in the chain, counted from zero. The block hash is the name of a block, made by hashing its header, and it cannot be chosen freely because proof of work demands many leading zeros. The median time is the middle timestamp of the last eleven blocks. The table at the bottom explains each term. A common mistake is to confuse height with confirmations: a block's height stays fixed, while its confirmations grow with every block added on top.",
45: "Height counts the blocks before a block, so the first block has height zero. Block 123,456 with five blocks on top shows the idea: each block points back to the one before it, and the tip, 123,461, has one confirmation, itself. Confirmations are the number of blocks from yours to the tip, yours included, so block 123,456 has six. Students often forget to count their own block. The numbers are example values, chosen to show the counting.",
46: "A block header has six fields and 80 bytes: 4, 32, 32, 4, 4 and 4. Block 123,456 was saved from a public API, and the code packs its header and hashes it twice with SHA-256. Reversing the byte order gives the block's own name, with its zeros in front, and it equals the saved id. The zeros appear because proof of work demanded them. A common mistake is to hash the whole block instead of the header, and to forget the reversal.",
47: "Block 123,456 holds 13 transactions. The code starts from their ids and hashes neighbours in pairs, level after level. The levels have 13, 7, 4, 2 and 1 hashes, because at an odd level the last hash is paired with itself. The single hash at the top is the merkle root, and it equals the root saved in the block. One changed transaction changes the root. A common mistake is to think the root lists the transactions; it is one hash that commits to all of them.",
48: "Miners write a time into every block header, and it can be a little off. The median time past is the sixth value of the eleven sorted timestamps of the blocks that end with block 123,456; its value is 1,305,197,900, the same as the median time saved in the block. A median is not moved by one wrong time, which an average would be. The rules use the median time past as a clock. In the chart the teal bar marks the sixth sorted value, and the bars show minutes since the first of the eleven blocks.",
49: "Two limits shape the chain. Block weight limits what a block may hold: a block without witness data weighs four times its size, and the limit is 4,000,000 weight units, which getblock reports beside size and strippedsize. The block subsidy limits the new coins a miner may claim: it starts at 50 bitcoins and is shifted right once for every 210,000 blocks, so block 775,072 lies in the fourth era and pays 6.25 bitcoins. A common mistake is to read the subsidy as the fee; fees are paid by the transactions in addition.",
50: "The bits field is a compact way to write the target. The four bytes 0x1a6a93b3 in the header give a length byte, 0x1a, which means 26 bytes, and three digits, 6a93b3. The target is those digits shifted left by 26 minus 3, which is 23 bytes, and a block hash must not exceed it. The expected number of hashes is 2 to the power 256 divided by the target plus one, about 6.8 times 10 to the power 14 for this block, and the chain work adds them up. A smaller target means more work; this block is 157,416.4 times harder than the easiest target.",
51: "A fork is a split of the chain into two branches, accidental or permanent. When two blocks are found at about the same time, nodes keep both branches until one gets more work. The best chain is the valid chain with the most cumulative proof of work, which is not always the one with the most blocks. A chain reorganization happens when a node switches to the chain with more work: blocks leave the best chain, and their transactions return to the mempool unless the new chain includes them. A common mistake is to count blocks instead of work.",
52: "Part 8 looks beyond Bitcoin Core, with two terms. The python-bitcoinlib library calls the node and builds transactions from Python, turning calls such as p.getblockcount() into JSON-RPC requests. An alternative client is another program that follows Bitcoin's rules; whether it agrees with Bitcoin Core on every rule has to be tested, not assumed. A common mistake is to assume that every client agrees with Core on every rule. The next slides show how a library reaches the node and how fresh nine toolkits are.",
53: "A library speaks to the node for you. The script asks for the block count, python-bitcoinlib builds the JSON-RPC call, HTTP carries it to the node, and bitcoind answers with the result, which the library returns to the script. The JSON-RPC call is therefore built in the library, not in your script. On the offline node of release 31.1 the call getblockcount returned 0, and python-bitcoinlib imported under Python 3.14.4, as the card records.",
54: "A wrapper library hides the HTTP call: p.getblockchaininfo() becomes a JSON-RPC request over HTTP, the node answers, and the library decodes the answer. The class RawProxy is the object that does this in python-bitcoinlib. A software library is a collection of code written to be used by other programs. A package registry is a public index of the libraries published for a language, with their versions and release dates, so it shows how fresh a library is before you rely on it. The next slides do exactly that for nine toolkits.",
55: "Toolkits age, and a package registry shows how long ago each was last released. The code counts the days from the latest release date of nine toolkits to the day of the query, 2026-10-02: python-bitcoinlib last released 1,217 days before, bitcoinjs-lib only 25. The chart uses a log scale, so bar height is not a simple ratio of days. An old library is not necessarily useless, but its age is a fact to weigh before it is used in a new project. A common mistake is to read bar heights on a log axis as a simple ratio.",
56: "Data from your own node is authoritative, because the node verified it itself, and this is the kind of fact that a provenance record states about any data. Plain JSON keys such as chain and blocks are ambiguous. A JSON-LD context maps each key to an IRI, an internationalized resource identifier that is a global name for a thing, which makes the JSON linked data without changing its shape. The example.org names on the slide are placeholders, not a real vocabulary. A context changes the meaning of the keys, not the data.",
57: "Four small computing ideas stand under the numbers of this chapter. A bit is a binary digit and a byte is eight bits, one of 256 values, written as two hexadecimal digits; the 32 bytes of the cookie are 64 hexadecimal characters. A bit shift moves the bits of an integer left or right, as v >> 29 does in the version and as the subsidy does once per halving. Unix time counts the seconds since 1 January 1970 at 00:00 UTC, and the median time 1,305,197,900 is one. A random number cannot be predicted, as the cookie and the salt are. A byte is two hexadecimal digits, not one.",
58: "Seven words from computing sit under the chapter. A file holds data under a name and a directory holds files, as the data directory does. A process is a running program with its own memory, and a thread is a flow of execution inside it; bitcoind and bitcoin-cli run as separate processes. A software library is code written for other programs, and a platform is the processor family and operating system a program is built for. A network protocol is an agreed format and order of messages, and software testing runs a program on chosen inputs and compares the results. C++ is the node's language; Python serves tests and examples.",
59: "The summary answers the six opening questions in one line each. Core is the reference because it came first, defines the rules by running them and is built in the open. A release is trusted by checking out a final tag, building it and comparing its hash with the published one. The settings decide what is stored, how much memory is used and which alerts run. A node is asked with JSON-RPC calls, and the cookie or an rpcauth credential lets a program in. A transaction is read by decoding it: the txid ignores the witness and the weight counts it. A block is checked by hashing the 80-byte header and rebuilding the merkle root.",
60: "The resources slide lists links that were opened on 25 October 2026 and are free to use. The chapter source in Mastering Bitcoin, the Bitcoin Core releases page and the RPC reference come first; they give the text this deck follows, every release with notes and signatures, and every call with its arguments and results. BIP 1 explains how a change is proposed and numbered, and Nakamoto's white paper has nine pages. The build guide for Unix lists dependencies and commands for bitcoind. The community links are the PR Review Club, Delving Bitcoin and Bitcoin Stack Exchange, and a talk on tracing Bitcoin Core v23.0 shows a developer watching a running node with tracepoints.",
61: "This is Bitcoin Core from the source code to a node that answers questions. Every result in the deck was computed by the chapter library under Python 3.14.6 and not typed in by hand. The six questions of slide 3 were answered in the summary: the reference implementation, the release and its checks, the settings, the interface and who may call it, the transaction and its two identifiers, and the block and its chain work. The next chapter, chapter 4, turns to keys and addresses, on which the node's wallet depends. Questions may be collected for the start of the next lecture.",
}

